  "worked_solution": "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{\u2032}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nFirst, we draw the phase line with the given information. \n\nNote that there are $2$ equilibrium points, $y=0$ and $y=-4.$\n\n- We're given that $y=0$ is a source. We represent this on the phase line by drawing an upward-pointing arrow just above $y=0$ and a downward-pointing arrow just below it.\n\n- We're given that $y=-4$ is a sink. We represent this on the phase line by drawing a downward-pointing arrow just above $y=-4$ (already drawn by the above property) and an upward-pointing arrow just below it.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042839/assets/e5ba765b9caffab904f07c5086d3206f72e36e66cb018673ce7ed44d79d19e88.png)\n\nOf the given options, the only slope field that correspond with this phase line is the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042839/assets/41f32a097325dbe2a278da2abe4ef93b13d058ffe49431882fade3bc40bc0f48.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- The slope field is horizontal at $y=0$ and $y=-4.$\n\n- The slopes near $y=0$ appear to diverge away from this line.\n\n- The slopes near $y=-4$ appear to converge toward this line.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
